  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=\u27e8t+\\frac{1}{t},\\,t-\\frac{1}{t}\u27e9$ at the point where $t=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "At the point where $t=1$,  the vector ${r}^{\u2032}(1)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\frac{1}{t})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e81-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\u27e9 \\\\ {r}^{\u2032}(1) & =\u27e81-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}},\\,1+\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e81-1,\\,1+1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e80,\\,2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=1$  is  $\u27e80,\\,2\u27e9.$",
